student_response:
I made a mistake.  When you divide by a number, you have to multiply by its reciprocal.  
So

\[
\frac{2}{8}\div 10 \;=\;\frac{2}{8}\div \frac{10}{1}
   \;=\;\frac{2}{8}\times\frac{1}{10}
   \;=\;\frac{2}{8\cdot10}
   \;=\;\frac{2}{80}
   \;=\;\frac{1}{40}.
\]

Thus the correct answer is \(\displaystyle \frac{1}{40}\).  Thanks for pointing out the error!

I didn’t realize I’d mis‑applied the rule.  
When we divide by a fraction we need to multiply by its reciprocal, not just mix the numerators and denominators.

So
\[
\frac{4}{8} \div \frac{7}{4}
= \frac{4}{8} \times \frac{4}{7}
= \frac{4\times 4}{8\times 7}
= \frac{16}{56}
= \frac{2}{7}\qquad(\text{after simplifying}).
\]

The correct answer is \(\boxed{\tfrac{2}{7}}\). I had mistakenly added the parts instead of using the reciprocal, which is why I got \(7/8\) before. Thank you for pointing that out!

