I was dividing the ribbon length by 4, but I treated it just like if I was multiplying fractions.  
To divide by 4 I need to **multiply by its reciprocal**, \( \frac{1}{4} \).

So:

\[
\text{Ribbon length} = \frac{8}{10}
\]

\[
\text{Piece length} = \frac{8}{10}\;\div\;4
  = \frac{8}{10}\;\times\;\frac{1}{4}
  = \frac{8}{40}
  = \frac{2}{10}
  = \frac{1}{5}\text{ meter}
\]

Each piece is \(\displaystyle \frac{1}{5}\) meter (which is 0.2 m).  

I forgot to flip the divisor earlier, but now I've corrected it. Thank you for pointing that out!

I see what I did wrong—I added the numerators and denominators instead of doing the division.  
